  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Businessman Brendan McAssey backed a research team investigating whether cells from the amniotic membrane could help patients recover. Through the Beluga Foundation, which he established with his wife, he funded an initial human safety trial.\n\nThe foundation subsequently financed the manufacture of enough cells for a larger trial across Australian hospitals. McAssey wanted to support work that had struggled to attract conventional funding. The grants gave researchers the means to test a potential treatment; they were part of his broader commitment to reducing the barriers faced by people with disabilities.",
    "html": "<p>Businessman Brendan McAssey backed a research team investigating whether cells from the amniotic membrane could help patients recover. Through the Beluga Foundation, which he established with his wife, he funded an initial human safety trial.</p>\r\n<p>The foundation subsequently financed the manufacture of enough cells for a larger trial across Australian hospitals. McAssey wanted to support work that had struggled to attract conventional funding. The grants gave researchers the means to test a potential treatment; they were part of his broader commitment to reducing the barriers faced by people with disabilities.</p>\r\n",
